 brothers were also on the bell, but the act that changed Jean's life was lower down the bell. A young singer named Elvis Presley.\"","year":1955,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 41: \"Be-Bop-A-Lula\" by Gene Vincent and the Bluecaps","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tYBQ5F7O3G0"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Influence on Holly's Brothers","body":"Buddy Holly's elder brothers performed as a country duo, adopting a style \"much the same\" as that of the Louvin Brothers. \"His elder brothers performed as a country duo in much the same style as the Louvain Brothers.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode Sixty-One: \"That'll Be the Day\", by the Crickets","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KVJMKLc54O0"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Musical Influence","body":"Johnny Cash, Luther Perkins, and Marshall Grant admired The Louvin Brothers and particularly wanted their early musical efforts to sound like them during their jam sessions. \"They were just singing stuff they'd heard on the radio, and they particularly wanted to sound like the Louvain Brothers.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 37: \"I Walk The Line\" by Johnny Cash","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1uhw6KjgSME"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Signed by Ken Nelson","body":"Ken Nelson, head of country music for Capitol Records, known for his impeccable taste in artists, signed The Louvin Brothers, among other classic country acts, to the label. \"He also signed Buck Owens and the Louvain Brothers among many other classic country artists...\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 41: \"Be-Bop-A-Lula\" by Gene Vincent and the Bluecaps","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tYBQ5F7O3G0"}]}],"awards":[],"concerts":[{"kind":"concert","title":"Country musi